       Question 2: What attack type was used to gain access to tge system?(one word)

- Decided to download wireshark and use it instead and it is much better since it is not limited in terms of resources.

-Since the conclusion was that the service used was SSH in the previous question, we use the name SSH on the filer on wireshark.

-We investigate the traffic on the SSH and notice that there are two IP adresses that have a suspisiously high traffic of SSH.

-The traffic follows the SSH protocol that consists of protocol, then Key Exchange Init, then Diffie-Hellman Group Exchange Request.

- What makes this alarming is the time difference between the traffic which are milliseconds apart. Normally, a user opens SSH one session at 
a time which makes this traffic look very suspicious.

- The conclusion is that the method used to gain access was Bruteforce attack. This type of attack involves checking all passwords or
combinations until the right one is found.


      Question 3: What was the tool the attacker possibly used to perform this attack?

- The conclusion was the the method used to gain access was brute force attack

- What we have to figure out is the name of the tool that is designed to automate the process of attempting multiple usernames and password
combinations against a target system at high speed until the correct comibation is found

- The name of this tool is called hydra. Hydra is a is a parallelized login cracker which supports numerous protocols to attack. 

- It is very fast and flexible, and new modules are easy to add. This tool is used by researchers to show the ease of gaining
unauthorised access to a system.

- What made the conclusion that the tool is hydra is when the invesigation of the multiple attempts with a span of milliseconds was done.
This behaviour of rapid SSH connection attempts is seen in the tool Hydra.


    Question 4: How many failed attempts were there?

- Information about attemps are usually encrypted so one cannot just read a list saying "Successful" or "Failed"

- Instead people analyze attemps by investigating packet count, coversation length and TCP stream behaviour.

- So in wireshark, we navigate to Statistics > Coversations and head over to the TCP tab.

- The TCP tab shows a long list which even shows the total number of attemps which are 54. But we are looking for the total number of failed attempts.

- In this case, we investigate the packet count. When an attempt is failed the number of packets ranges from 26 - 28 but when an attempt is successful,
the total number of packets is more than 28.

- We only need to check the packet count and find the attempts that are more than the 26-28 range and we see there are two attempts consisting of
 50 and 468 packets. We remove the two packets and remain with 52 attemps which can be concluded as failed attempts.



